{"reference":"2 Samuel 7","book":"2 Samuel","chapter":7,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"David proposes building a permanent house for the ark, but the LORD redirects the plan: David will not build that house; instead, the LORD will make David a “house,” meaning an enduring royal line. The promise concerns David’s offspring and throne, holds together father-son language, discipline for wrongdoing, and continuing covenant loyalty. The chapter therefore does not present an unqualified guarantee that removes judgment from David’s line.","verse_refs":["2 Samuel 7:1-29"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_samuel_007_766fa504b745507c8f52cc11"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A later explanation","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Nathan’s oracle does not explain why David is not to build the temple. Chronicles later gives an explanation, saying that David’s warfare and bloodshed disqualified him from that task. Reading the accounts together requires preserving this difference: the reason is stated in Chronicles, not in 2 Samuel 7 itself.","verse_refs":["2 Samuel 7:1-17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_samuel_007_50f5880cfc0b3db30e045ca4"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The wider setting","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"For the surrounding account of David’s kingdom, archaeology supports the existence of a later Davidic dynasty. It does not, however, establish one uncontested picture of the size, administration, or chronology of David’s own kingdom.","verse_refs":["2 Samuel 5","2 Samuel 6:1-23","2 Samuel 8:1-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_samuel_007_103c811bb5f19aef89aac529"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"Within the larger narrative, this promise follows David’s accession, kingdom consolidation, and the ark’s arrival. The book later moves into royal abuse, violence within David’s household, rebellion, grief, and a closing appendix. That wider movement places the dynasty promise before the account’s later portrayals of royal failure and conflict.","verse_refs":["2 Samuel 1","2 Samuel 10:1-19","2 Samuel 11:1-27","2 Samuel 12:1-31","2 Samuel 13:1-39","2 Samuel 14:1-33","2 Samuel 15:1-37","2 Samuel 16:1-23","2 Samuel 17:1-29","2 Samuel 18:1-33","2 Samuel 19:1-43","2 Samuel 20:1-26","2 Samuel 21:1-22","2 Samuel 22:1-51","2 Samuel 23:1-39","2 Samuel 24:1-25","2 Samuel 2:1-32","2 Samuel 3:1-39","2 Samuel 4:1-12","2 Samuel 5:1-25","2 Samuel 6:1-23","2 Samuel 8:1-18","2 Samuel 9:1-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_samuel_007_123c5e9d4fc3b27800be08d3"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}